So far we've used this format in source code: `Device &somedevice`.

```cpp
plugins/follow_me/follow_me.h:    const Config &get_config() const;
plugins/follow_me/follow_me.h:    const TargetLocation &get_last_location() const;
plugins/follow_me/follow_me_impl.h:    const FollowMe::Config &get_config() const;
plugins/follow_me/follow_me_impl.h:    const FollowMe::TargetLocation &get_last_location() const;core/device.h:    Time &get_time() { return _time; };
core/device.h:    DroneCoreImpl &_parent;
core/dronecore_impl.h:    Device &get_device();
core/dronecore_impl.h:    Device &get_device(uint64_t uuid);
core/dronecore_impl.h:    const std::vector<uint64_t> &get_device_uuids() const;
core/dronecore.h:    const std::vector<uint64_t> &device_uuids() const;
core/mavlink_receiver.h:    mavlink_message_t &get_last_message()
core/mavlink_receiver.h:    mavlink_status_t &get_status()
...
```
I think, even `Device& somedevice` also fine: It says `somedevice` is of type `Device&`.
But `Device & somedevice` is hard to read. Isn't it ?

